# Database-per-Tenant Isolation — CRITICAL

## Topology
Vokit has a control plane plus tenant data planes.

Control plane:
- platform/global metadata;
- platform users/RBAC;
- tenant registry;
- tenant lifecycle/status;
- tenant database endpoint metadata and credential references;
- provider/global configuration;
- cross-tenant operational indexes that are explicitly approved;
- audit/security metadata that must survive tenant DB loss.

Tenant data plane:
- tenant business data and child customer scope;
- agents, numbers, calls, usage, integrations, knowledge metadata, tenant users and operational records as approved.

## Tenant definition
Default V1 topology: an Agency is the database tenant boundary; Customer remains a mandatory child authorization scope inside that tenant.
- Do not create one database per customer unless an ADR explicitly changes this topology.
- A database tenant MUST have a stable immutable tenant_id and database_id.
- Never derive database identity from untrusted URL/query/body input.

## Remote MySQL requirement
Each tenant database may be on a remote MySQL host.
The platform MUST maintain a tenant database registry containing:
- tenant_id;
- database_id/name;
- host;
- port;
- TLS requirements/certificate reference;
- secret/credential reference;
- status;
- schema version;
- last health check;
- provisioning state;
- migration state;
- region/location metadata if applicable.

Secrets:
- store only encrypted secret references, never plaintext passwords in business rows;
- never log DSNs, passwords, auth tokens or private keys;
- never expose DB connection details to React or tenant users;
- never serialize DSNs into error responses.

## Connection routing
- Resolve tenant context before opening tenant DB access.
- Acquire connection configuration only from trusted server-side tenant registry.
- Bind connection selection to an authenticated authorization context, not a request parameter alone.
- Connection pools must be tenant-safe. Never reuse a pooled connection across tenant identities.
- Prefer short-lived or bounded pools per active tenant and enforce pool/connection limits.
- Every tenant DB connection must use TLS where supported/required by environment policy.
- Reject disabled/suspended/unknown tenant database routes.
- Never fall back to a default/shared tenant DB for a failed tenant lookup.

## Fail-closed rules
- Missing tenant mapping => deny.
- Missing credentials => deny.
- Unhealthy/unknown database => fail closed for writes; read behavior must be explicitly designed.
- Cross-tenant DB handle => terminate request/job and emit security telemetry.
- Never query another tenant DB as a fallback.
- Never "try all databases until a record is found."

## Background jobs
Every asynchronous job must carry immutable tenant context:
- tenant_id;
- customer_id when applicable;
- actor/system identity;
- correlation_id;
- event/job ID.

Workers must reconstruct DB routing from the trusted tenant registry on execution. Do not serialize raw credentials into jobs.

## Migrations
Tenant schema migrations are not a single global migration command.
Implement:
- control-plane migrations;
- tenant schema version tracking;
- resumable tenant migration jobs;
- bounded concurrency;
- per-tenant migration lock;
- retry/reconciliation;
- canary tenant rollout;
- failure isolation;
- migration observability.

Never assume all tenant databases are simultaneously online.

## Query rules
- Tenant repositories must bind to the resolved tenant DB.
- Customer-scoped records additionally require customer_id authorization.
- Platform-wide queries may use only approved control-plane projections/indexes or explicit privileged tenant traversal.
- Never join across tenant databases inside normal request paths.

## Testing
Required tests include:
- tenant A cannot read/write tenant B;
- same object ID in two tenant DBs resolves only in current tenant;
- forged tenant_id cannot switch DB routing;
- worker jobs cannot cross tenant boundaries;
- failed tenant DB resolution fails closed;
- connection pools cannot leak tenant context;
- tenant DB outage does not route traffic to another tenant;
- migration failure isolates one tenant and is observable.
